<worksheet>
    <title>LT 12 Introduction to Logarithms and Solving</title>
    <introduction>
        <p align="center">
            <m>b^x=y</m> is equivalent to <m>x=\log_b(y)</m>.
        </p>
        <p align="center">
            The logarithm is the inverse of the exponential function.
        </p>
        <p align="center">
            The logarithm is the exponent to which the base must be raised to produce a given number.
        </p>
        <p align="center">
            For example, <m>\log_2(8)=3</m> because <m>2^3=8</m>.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                First evaluate the log equation using Desmos when needed. Then complete the rest of each row.
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <tabular top="minor" bottom="medium" left="major" right="major">
            <col width="20%" halign="center"/>
            <col width="20%" halign="center"/>
            <col width="20%" halign="center"/>
            <col width="20%" halign="center"/>
            <row header="yes" halign="center">
                <cell>Logarithm</cell>
                <cell>Base</cell>
                <cell>Argument (answer of an exponential)</cell>
                <cell>Exponential Form</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
                <cell><m>\log_2(16)=</m>_________</cell>
                <cell></cell>
                <cell></cell>
                <cell></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
                <cell><m>\log_6(1)=</m>_________</cell>
                <cell></cell>
                <cell></cell>
                <cell></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
                <cell><m>\log_3(27)=</m>_________</cell>
                <cell></cell>
                <cell></cell>
                <cell></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
                <cell><m>\log_8(\sqrt{8})</m>=_________</cell>
                <cell></cell>
                <cell></cell>
                <cell></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
                <cell><m>\log_5\left(\frac{1}{125}\right)</m>=_________</cell>
                <cell></cell>
                <cell></cell>
                <cell></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
                <cell><m>\log (100)</m>=_________ (common log)</cell>
                <cell></cell>
                <cell></cell>
                <cell></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
                <cell><m>\ln(e)</m>=_______________ (natural log)</cell>
                <cell></cell>
                <cell></cell>
                <cell></cell>
            </row>
        </tabular>
    </exercise>
     <exercise>
        <introduction>
        <p>
            The most important phrase in learning logs are _________________.
        </p>
        <p>
            Keeping in mind that logs are exponents, convert these exponentials to logs.
        </p>
        </introduction>
        <task >
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>3^x=16</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>(0.25)^t=7</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
       
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>e^{x-8}=6</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>
